**Goal** — Extract the hardened approval-memory policy into nax-agent's public entry so multiple front doors share one policy.

**Motivation** — "Always allow" must not fork.

**Approach** — **Approval memory in nax-agent (US-002, US-003).** New `packages/nax-agent/src/permissions/approval-memory.ts` exports `approvalMemoryKey(request)` and `approvalMemoryApplies(request, decision)`, carrying `memoryKey` and `memoryApplies` unchanged (same wrappers list, plain-word rule, mask marker and shell metacharacters) except that the execute-tool test is a local set `APPROVAL_EXECUTE_TOOLS` = `Bash`, `RunCommand`, `Git`, `GitCommit` (the tools the ACP `toolKind` maps to `"execute"`). Both take `ApprovalMemoryRequest = { readonly tool: string; readonly command?: string }`, which an `approval_requested` event satisfies, and are exported from the package entry. The ACP server's `permissions.ts` then calls them and deletes its copies (`memoryKey`, `memoryApplies`, `WRAPPERS`, `PLAIN_WORD`, `MASK_MARKER`, `SHELL_METACHARACTERS`); `toolKind` stays in the ACP server for display.

Create `src/permissions/approval-memory.ts` with `approvalMemoryKey` and `approvalMemoryApplies` carrying the ACP logic; export both and the two types through `src/permissions/index.ts` and `src/index.ts`; port the ACP `memoryKey` cases. Regenerate the API snapshot with `bun run api:update` so it gains approvalMemoryKey, approvalMemoryApplies, type ApprovalMemoryRequest and type ApprovalMemoryDecision. Preserve wrapper basename/case handling, flag-second-word refusal, non-plain/masked key refusal, and the distinction between key construction and deciding whether an allow applies to a later command.

**Scope** — In: shared pure policy, public/barrel exports, API snapshot and ported unit cases. Out: replacing ACP callers/removing their local helpers is US-003; terminal memory ownership is US-007.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface ApprovalMemoryRequest { readonly tool: string; readonly command?: string }
export type ApprovalMemoryDecision = "allow" | "deny";
export function approvalMemoryKey(request: ApprovalMemoryRequest): string | undefined;
export function approvalMemoryApplies(request: ApprovalMemoryRequest, decision: ApprovalMemoryDecision): boolean;
```

**Goal** — Make the ACP broker consume the public shared approval-memory policy without altering permission round trips.

**Motivation** — The ACP server's per-session approval memory is security-sensitive.

**Approach** — **Approval memory in nax-agent (US-002, US-003).** New `packages/nax-agent/src/permissions/approval-memory.ts` exports `approvalMemoryKey(request)` and `approvalMemoryApplies(request, decision)`, carrying `memoryKey` and `memoryApplies` unchanged (same wrappers list, plain-word rule, mask marker and shell metacharacters) except that the execute-tool test is a local set `APPROVAL_EXECUTE_TOOLS` = `Bash`, `RunCommand`, `Git`, `GitCommit` (the tools the ACP `toolKind` maps to `"execute"`). Both take `ApprovalMemoryRequest = { readonly tool: string; readonly command?: string }`, which an `approval_requested` event satisfies, and are exported from the package entry. The ACP server's `permissions.ts` then calls them and deletes its copies (`memoryKey`, `memoryApplies`, `WRAPPERS`, `PLAIN_WORD`, `MASK_MARKER`, `SHELL_METACHARACTERS`); `toolKind` stays in the ACP server for display.

`src/server/permissions.ts` calls the helpers from `@nathapp/nax-agent` and deletes `memoryKey`, `memoryApplies` and their constants; the `memoryKey` describe block leaves `test/unit/server/permissions.test.ts`. Its cases now live in nax-agent's approval-memory.test.ts from US-002 while every createPermissionBroker case stays unchanged and passing. Verify removals using package typecheck/check:all as implementation validation, not behavioral ACs. Add the broker seam assertions and parity check across every execute tool.

**Scope** — In: ACP imports/call sites, removal of the copied policy and migrated describe, preserved broker behavior. Out: shared-helper implementation/API snapshot is US-002; terminal asks are US-007. The ACP server's permission round trips, allow_always/reject_always options and broker behaviour are unchanged.

**Interface** — Consume the US-002 declarations from the package entry:
```typescript
export interface ApprovalMemoryRequest { readonly tool: string; readonly command?: string }
export type ApprovalMemoryDecision = "allow" | "deny";
export function approvalMemoryKey(request: ApprovalMemoryRequest): string | undefined;
export function approvalMemoryApplies(request: ApprovalMemoryRequest, decision: ApprovalMemoryDecision): boolean;
```

**Goal** — Compose the native opener, rule handlers, asks, fallback, ledger and storage behind the headless ChatHost.

**Motivation** — One agent for the whole loop.

**Approach** — **The host (US-010 core, US-011 resume and switch).** `createChatHost(deps)` in `packages/nax/src/chat/chat-host.ts` is the composition root the shell drives. `deps`: `config`, `cwd`, `repoRoot`, `chain` (an `InteractionChain` or null), `open` (defaults to `openChatSession`), `storage`, `now`, `logger`. It creates one rule-delivery handler set (`createRuleDeliveryHandlers`) and one ledger per host and reuses them across reopens, one ask controller, and one fallback over the controller and `chain` with `config.interaction.defaults.chatAskFallbackMs`.
- `send` runs `runChatTurn` on the current handle and stores the returned meta, so `current().meta.costUsd` is the session spend.
- `listSessions` maps `storage.list({ cwd })` with `heldByPid` set when `readLockHolder` reports `live`.
- `start`, `resume` and `clear` while a session is open open the new session first, then close the old one and release its lock; if the open fails, the old session stays open and current, and the error is rethrown. `clear()` and a resume of a different id call `asks.reset()`, so a remembered decision never crosses sessions. The fallback and the chain survive `clear` and `resume`.
- `close()` is final: it disposes the fallback, closes the session, releases the lock and calls `chain.destroy()` when a chain is present.
- `models` lists `models.native` `fast`, `balanced` and `powerful`, skipping absent tiers; a string entry is used as is, a `ModelDef` entry as `<provider>/<model>`.

The host uses 60000 for chatAskFallbackMs when the optional hand-written config field is absent. Derive projectKey with getProjectKey(config, repoRoot) and outputDir with projectOutputDir(projectKey, config.outputDir). Keep answer dynamically bound to the current session. Export the host surface through src/chat/index.ts.

All ACs build the host over `openChatSession` with `_openChatSessionDeps` fakes, a real storage over a temp directory, and a fake chain.

**Scope** — In: composition, start/clear/send/cancel/current/models/repoRoot/listSessions/close and preserving old current session on failed replacement. Out: resume/latest/notice and transactional mode/model switches are US-011; the full interface below is the end-state contract, not a requirement to implement US-011 here. Ink is US-012 onward. No SessionManager dispatch or budget enforcement.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface ChatHost {
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly models: readonly string[]; // models.native fast/balanced/powerful, deduplicated
  readonly asks: ChatAskController;
  current(): ChatHostState; // { sessionId, mode, model, meta, sandbox? }
  notice(): string | undefined;
  start(input: { mode: ChatMode; model: string }): Promise<void>;
  clear(): Promise<void>; // new session, same mode and model
  resume(input: { id?: string }): Promise<void>;
  send(message: string, onEvent: (event: SessionEvent) => void): Promise<void>;
  cancel(): void;
  switchTo(input: { mode?: ChatMode; model?: string }): Promise<void>;
  listSessions(): Promise<readonly ChatSessionRow[]>; // { sessionId, title, updatedAt, heldByPid? }
  close(): Promise<void>;
}
```

**Goal** — Add shared-session resume and lock-preserving, rollback-safe mode/model switching to ChatHost.

**Motivation** — koda must see the same sessions.

**Approach** — **The host (US-010 core, US-011 resume and switch).** `createChatHost(deps)` in `packages/nax/src/chat/chat-host.ts` is the composition root the shell drives. `deps`: `config`, `cwd`, `repoRoot`, `chain` (an `InteractionChain` or null), `open` (defaults to `openChatSession`), `storage`, `now`, `logger`. It creates one rule-delivery handler set (`createRuleDeliveryHandlers`) and one ledger per host and reuses them across reopens, one ask controller, and one fallback over the controller and `chain` with `config.interaction.defaults.chatAskFallbackMs`.
- `resume({})` walks `storage.list({ cwd })` in order and opens the first entry whose metadata reads, whose mode `chatModeOfMeta` accepts, and whose lock `readLockHolder` does not report `live`; none -> `CHAT_SESSION_NOT_FOUND` with message `no resumable session for <cwd>`. `resume({ id })` opens that id; when its `acquireLock` rejects with `SESSION_LOCKED`, the host reads `storage.readLockHolder(id)` and throws `CHAT_SESSION_LOCKED` with message `session <id> is in use by pid <pid>`.
- After a resume whose `session.lastTurn?.status` is `interrupted`, `notice()` is `The last turn was interrupted. Send a message to continue it.`
- `switchTo` between turns: `session.close()` (the lock is kept), then reopen the same id with the new settings, write the metadata's `mode`, `bashApproval` and `model`, and call `asks.setMode`. If the reopen throws, the old settings are reopened and the error is rethrown; the metadata keeps the old settings.
- `start`, `resume` and `clear` while a session is open open the new session first, then close the old one and release its lock; if the open fails, the old session stays open and current, and the error is rethrown. `clear()` and a resume of a different id call `asks.reset()`, so a remembered decision never crosses sessions. The fallback and the chain survive `clear` and `resume`.

Explicit corrupt metadata propagates SESSION_META_UNREADABLE without taking a lock; resume-latest skips unreadable entries. An explicit missing id produces CHAT_SESSION_NOT_FOUND with message no session <id> in <dir>; none/read modes are refused before locking with CHAT_SESSION_MODE_UNSUPPORTED and message session <id> was created in mode <mode>; nax chat opens full or ask sessions only. A plain resume preserves the stored model/mode. Same-session switches preserve history, handler identity and approval memory.

All ACs build the host over `openChatSession` with `_openChatSessionDeps` fakes and a real storage over a temp directory.

**Scope** — In: resume, notice, switchTo, metadata commit/rollback and session-replacement failure behavior in the existing host. Out: core host creation is US-010; shell queueing Shift-Tab until turn_end is US-013. A resumed session does not show which mode each earlier turn ran under; metadata holds the current mode only. No new SIGKILL recovery.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface ChatHost {
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly models: readonly string[]; // models.native fast/balanced/powerful, deduplicated
  readonly asks: ChatAskController;
  current(): ChatHostState; // { sessionId, mode, model, meta, sandbox? }
  notice(): string | undefined;
  start(input: { mode: ChatMode; model: string }): Promise<void>;
  clear(): Promise<void>; // new session, same mode and model
  resume(input: { id?: string }): Promise<void>;
  send(message: string, onEvent: (event: SessionEvent) => void): Promise<void>;
  cancel(): void;
  switchTo(input: { mode?: ChatMode; model?: string }): Promise<void>;
  listSessions(): Promise<readonly ChatSessionRow[]>; // { sessionId, title, updatedAt, heldByPid? }
  close(): Promise<void>;
}
```
